## Problem

Compute $\pi_1(S^3 \setminus \gamma)$, where $\gamma$ is a (geometric) circle in $\mathbb{R}^3$ and $S^3 = \mathbb{R}^3 \cup \{\infty\}$.

## Proof

**Step 1. $\gamma$ is an unknotted embedded $S^1$ in $S^3$.**

A geometric circle in $\mathbb{R}^3$ (the image of the standard round $S^1$ under a rigid motion) bounds an embedded disk $D \subset \mathbb{R}^3 \subset S^3$: namely the flat disk spanning it. Hence $\gamma$ is the **unknot** in $S^3$.

**Step 2. Reduce to the knot exterior.**

Let $N(\gamma) \cong S^1 \times D^2$ be a closed tubular neighborhood of $\gamma$ in $S^3$. The complement
$$S^3 \setminus \gamma \;\supset\; S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int} N(\gamma)$$
is a deformation retract: radially push every point of $N(\gamma)\setminus\gamma$ outward to $\partial N(\gamma) = S^1 \times S^1$, keeping $S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int}N(\gamma)$ fixed. Concretely, using coordinates $(\theta, r, \phi)$ on $N(\gamma)\cong S^1\times D^2$ with $r\in[0,1]$, the retraction sends $r\mapsto 1$ while fixing $\theta,\phi$. Thus
$$\pi_1(S^3\setminus\gamma)\;\cong\;\pi_1\!\bigl(S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(\gamma)\bigr).$$

**Step 3. The exterior of the unknot is a solid torus.**

Since $\gamma$ bounds the embedded disk $D$, the pair $(S^3, \gamma)$ is the standard unknot. The genus-1 Heegaard splitting of $S^3$ writes
$$S^3 \;=\; \underbrace{N(\gamma)}_{\text{solid torus } S^1\times D^2}\;\cup_{S^1\times S^1}\;\underbrace{S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(\gamma)}_{\text{solid torus } S^1\times D^2},$$
i.e. $S^3$ is two solid tori glued along their common boundary torus, with $\gamma$ the core of the first. Therefore the exterior
$$S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(\gamma)\;\cong\;S^1\times D^2$$
is itself a solid torus.

*(Direct verification: a regular neighborhood of the spanning disk $D$ is a 3-ball $B^3$; its boundary sphere meets $\partial N(\gamma)$ in a single essential curve, so $S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(\gamma) \cong B^3$ with a 1-handle attached along that curve — a solid torus.)*

**Step 4. Compute the fundamental group.**

A solid torus deformation retracts onto its core circle:
$$S^1\times D^2 \;\simeq\; S^1.$$
Hence
$$\pi_1(S^3\setminus\gamma)\;\cong\;\pi_1(S^1\times D^2)\;\cong\;\pi_1(S^1)\;\cong\;\mathbb{Z}.$$

The generator is the meridian of $\gamma$ (a small loop linking $\gamma$ once), and the longitude is null-homotopic in the solid torus exterior — consistent with $\gamma$ being unknotted (Seifert genus $0$).

## Result

$$\boxed{\pi_1\!\bigl(S^3\setminus\gamma\bigr)\;\cong\;\mathbb{Z}.}$$

